    <item>
      <title>Daily Podcast 2026-08-05</title>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://github.com/vllorens/microbiome_digest/releases/download/episode-2026-08-05/podcast_2026-08-05.mp3</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 08:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://github.com/vllorens/microbiome_digest/releases/download/episode-2026-08-05/podcast_2026-08-05.mp3" length="19213268" type="audio/mpeg" />
      <description>Gut microbiota-derived deoxycholic acid fuels an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment that drives breast cancer progression, according to a new study in Cell Metabolism. | Endometriosis—often abbreviated as EMs—is a chronic, estrogen‑dependent systemic inflammatory disorder characterized by the ectopic implantation of endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus. | The paper’s title signals a focus on recent insights linking the gut microbiota to endometriosis pathogenesis, suggesting that researchers are exploring how microbial communities in the gastrointestinal tract might influence immune modulation, hormone metabolism, or inflammatory pathways that contribute to lesion establishment.</description>
      <itunes:author>Veronica Llorens</itunes:author>
      <itunes:summary>Gut microbiota-derived deoxycholic acid fuels an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment that drives breast cancer progression, according to a new study in Cell Metabolism. | Endometriosis—often abbreviated as EMs—is a chronic, estrogen‑dependent systemic inflammatory disorder characterized by the ectopic implantation of endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus. | The paper’s title signals a focus on recent insights linking the gut microbiota to endometriosis pathogenesis, suggesting that researchers are exploring how microbial communities in the gastrointestinal tract might influence immune modulation, hormone metabolism, or inflammatory pathways that contribute to lesion establishment.</itunes:summary>
      <itunes:explicit>false</itunes:explicit>
